<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<!DOCTYPE plist PUBLIC "-//Apple//DTD PLIST 1.0//EN" "http://www.apple.com/DTDs/PropertyList-1.0.dtd">
<plist version="1.0">
<dict>
  <!--
    Hardened-runtime entitlements for the signed, notarized, direct-sale
    snapsift.app (not the App Store; not sandboxed — see the owner's ruling
    of 2026-08-02 in the repo's task history). Consumed by the family
    signing pipeline, cver-tools' mac-release/release.sh, via its
    --entitlements flag; this file does no signing itself.

    Starting point: the family template at
    tools-lanes/.../mac-release/entitlements/direct-sale.entitlements. Same
    baseline reasoning applies here — com.apple.security.app-sandbox is
    deliberately ABSENT (that IS "not sandboxed"), and hardened runtime's
    defaults (no JIT, no unsigned executable memory, no DYLD_* injection, no
    debugger attach, no loading of dylibs/frameworks not signed by the same
    Team ID, no sending Apple events to unrelated processes) are all left in
    force below.

    Sparkle-specific finding: Sparkle 2's sandbox-escape machinery — its
    Installer.xpc / Downloader.xpc XPC services, and the
    temporary-exception.mach-lookup / temporary-exception.shared-preference
    entitlements that go with them (see Sparkle's own
    TestApplication/Sparkle-Test-App.entitlements) — exists ONLY to let a
    SANDBOXED host app reach outside its sandbox to install an update.
    snapsift is not sandboxed, so there is no sandbox boundary for Sparkle to
    cross: it runs its updater and relaunch helper (Autoupdate) as an
    ordinary child process, same as any other non-sandboxed app spawning a
    signed helper it ships. None of the sandboxed-app entitlements apply
    here, and adding them would be the same inert cargo-culting the family
    template already warns against.

    Library validation (the one hardened-runtime restriction Sparkle usually
    needs an exception for) is handled by SIGNING, not an entitlement:
    release.sh's `codesign --deep` re-signs Sparkle.framework and its nested
    Autoupdate.app with snapsift's OWN Developer ID Application identity
    before notarization, so every nested binary carries the same Team ID the
    hardened runtime checks for. com.apple.security.cs.disable-library-validation
    is not needed unless that deep-sign step is ever skipped for Sparkle's
    nested code — if it is, add the key here and say why, per the family
    template's rule.

    Net result, as of 2026-09-15: zero additional keys. Add one only when a
    real codesign/notarize/launch rejection names it, with a comment here
    explaining which Sparkle mechanism needed it.
  -->
</dict>
</plist>
